        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Waterproofing Cinder Block
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the foil test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealing a block wall that only sweats does nothing, because condensation forms on the room side of the block. Tape foil or plastic to the wall for a day or two before buying anything. If the moisture is on the room side, a dehumidifier and better air movement solve the problem for far less money than a sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling the first coat onto block</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A roller bridges the pinholes and voids in block instead of filling them, and each one becomes a path for water. Scrub the first coat of any masonry waterproofer into the wall with a stiff brush, working the product into mortar joints, then roll or brush the second coat once the pores are closed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over efflorescence or paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The white salt crust and old paint both stop a sealer from bonding or soaking in, and the new coat will flake off with them. Brush and clean efflorescence away, strip paint, and splash-test the wall. Any area where water still beads instead of darkening the block needs more prep.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a repellent against soil water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Silane-siloxane repellents shed rain beautifully on exterior block, but they are not built to stop water pushed through a basement wall from saturated soil. On the inside of a seeping basement wall, use a masonry waterproofer rated for hydrostatic pressure and fix gutters and grading outside first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a cracked or bowing wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stair-step cracks, horizontal cracks and inward bowing in block are signs of soil pressure on the structure, not just a water path. A fresh coating hides them while the force keeps working. Have a structural engineer inspect the wall before you seal it, since the repair may need anchors or bracing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best way to seal cinder blocks to avoid moisture?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Find the water source first with a foil test, then fix gutters and grading outside. On a basement wall that seeps, scrub two coats of a masonry waterproofer into bare, clean block. On exterior, garage or garden walls hit by rain, spray on a silane-siloxane repellent. Clean off efflorescence and paint before either, and patch active leaks with hydraulic cement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of sealer is best for waterproofing cinder block?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the side the water comes from. Cement-and-latex masonry waterproofers, the Drylok-type products, suit interior basement block because they resist water pressure to a labeled rating. Silane-siloxane penetrating repellents suit exterior above-grade block. Silicate densifiers are best on bare poured concrete, and clear acrylics are mainly cosmetic for dry, sheltered walls.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do silicate sealers work on basement walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Silicate sealers work by reacting with lime inside bare concrete, so they suit clean, unpainted poured walls best and perform less predictably on hollow block. They will not react through paint, old sealers or a layer of dirt and dust, so scrub and rinse the wall first. They reduce dampness but are not designed to stop heavy seepage from saturated soil.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What product seals a basement wall from inside the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A cement-based masonry waterproofer brushed onto bare block is the standard interior product. Makers rate these coatings for hydrostatic pressure; DRYLOK Extreme, for example, is rated by its manufacturer for 15 psi. Hydraulic cement handles active leaks and holes before coating. Ordinary latex paint, clear acrylics and repellents are not substitutes on a seeping basement wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you waterproof cinder block from the inside only?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Interior sealing handles dampness and slow seepage well once roof water and grading are under control, and it avoids excavation. It cannot stop water entering at the joint between wall and floor or hold back heavy pressure. If coatings blister or water pools along the floor after storms, the wall needs interior drain tile or exterior work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a sealed cinder block wall stay waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Labels on silane-siloxane repellents commonly quote around five to ten years in typical exposure, and interior masonry waterproofers usually last a similar period before touch-ups. Sunny, wet exterior walls wear faster than sheltered ones. Splash water on a repellent-treated wall each year; when it soaks in instead of beading, clean the wall and reapply.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over a sealed cinder block wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the sealer. Masonry waterproofers are designed to be the finish or to take a latex topcoat after they cure, and most come in tintable colors. Many silane-siloxane repellents leave a surface that paint will not grip, so check the data sheet before sealing. If you want color, choose a waterproofer or a block filler and masonry paint system instead.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

