          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Waterproofing a cinder block wall starts with the foil test and the gutters, not the sealer. Once you know the water is coming through the block, clean off efflorescence and paint, patch leaks with hydraulic cement, repoint bad joints, and then choose by side: a masonry waterproofer scrubbed into interior basement block, a silane-siloxane repellent on exterior, garage and garden walls.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If a coated wall blisters, water pools at the floor edge, or the block shows stair-step or horizontal cracks, stop sealing and look at drainage or structure instead. For specific products in each family, see our <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/">cinder block sealer reviews</a>.
